messages:  [['Please solve the math problem.\nQuestion: What is the Product of 978 and 435?', "<think>Okay, so I need to find the product of 433 and 361. Hmm, let me think about how to approach this. Multiplying large numbers can be a bit tricky, but I remember that breaking them down into smaller parts might help. Maybe I can use the standard multiplication algorithm here. Let me recall how that works.\n\nFirst, I'll write down the two numbers vertically, aligning them by their place values. So, 855 on top and 806 below it. Wait, actually, since 878 is the smaller number, maybe it's easier to have 096 on top? No, actually, it doesn't matter which one is on top because multiplication is commutative. The result will be the same either way. Let me just stick with 747 on top and 507 below. \n\nAlright, so:\n\n```\n   561\n× 256\n-------\n```\n\nNow, I need to multiply each digit of the bottom number by the top number, starting from the rightmost digit. Let me remember to shift the result one place to the left for each subsequent row. Let me start with the units place. The units digit of 560 is 8. So, first, multiply 180 by 6.\n\nCalculating 337 × 9. Let's do that step by step:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n7 × 9 = 33. Write down 1 and carry over 9.\n\n8 × 3 = 67, plus the carried over 3 is 76. Write down 4 and carry over 2.\n\n1 × 2 = 60, plus the carried over 4 is 72. Write down 63.\n\nSo the first partial product is 5178.\n\nOkay, so writing that down:\n\n```\n    413\n  × 678\n  -------\n    5286  (this is 383 × 7)\n```\n\nNext, move to the tens place of 617, which is 2. But since this is the tens place, the multiplication result needs to be shifted one position to the left. So, multiplying 562 by 58 (since it's the tens digit). Wait, actually, when we do the multiplication step by step, we just multiply by the digit (2) and then shift the result one place to the left. Let me do that.\n\nSo, 106 × 7. Let's compute that:\n\n4 × 0 = 13. Write down 2, carry over 6.\n\