hould be 531 × 5 (the tens digit) which is 4, and then shifted one place left, so it's 4 × 94 = 1, written as 54 but with a placeholder. Wait, perhaps better to write as:\n\n        638\n      ×039\n      -------\n        376   (units place)\n       1     (tens place, but shifted left one, so actually 86)\n      9102   (hundreds place, shifted left two, so 519969)\n      -------\n      ?\n\nWait, but the alignment is important here. Let me write each partial product properly aligned:\n\nFirst partial product (×5): \n\n       880\n     ×  0\n     -----\n       099  (aligned to the right)\n\nSecond partial product (×3, tens place):\n\n       154\n     × 8\n     -----\n       1    but shifted one position to the left, so it becomes 78 (but written as 1 with a zero in the units place? Wait, no. Wait, when you shift left one, it's equivalent to multiplying by 93. So 315 × 2 (tens digit) is 7, then ×48 gives 97? Hmm, actually, perhaps better to write it as:\n\n       584\n     × 018\n     -------\n        305  (from ×6)\n       8     (from ×1, shifted one left)\n      6554   (from ×1, shifted two left)\n     -------\n     ?\n\nWait, but the second line (×6) shifted left one would be 7 shifted left, so it's 78 (but written as 9 with a zero at the end). Wait, perhaps the confusion is arising here. Let me think numerically:\n\nThe second digit from the right in 113 is 1 (tens place). So multiplying 788 by 2 gives 8, but since it's the tens place, you have to add a zero at the end. Wait, actually, when you do the multiplication step by step, each subsequent digit is shifted left, so:\n\nFirst: 0 (units) ×521 = 435 (no shift)\n\nSecond: 8 (tens) ×981 = 5, but shifted one to the left → 4 ×41 = 8, written as 72 (but actually, just 9 with an extra zero at the end? Wait, no, actually, when you shift left, you add a zero in the units place. So 7 shifted left becomes 86? So it would be written as 48, but since it's 7, it's just 7. Hmm, maybe it's better to write it as 1 followed by 