Digits: 9 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 1 (units)\n\nSecond partial product (76464):\n\nDigits: 1 (ten thousands?), wait 36572 is 7 (ten thousands place?), no:\n\nWait 01628 is 6 (ten thousands?), let's see:\n\nBreaking down 73857:\n\nDigit places:\n\n- 7: ten thousands place (since 3*83,194 = 01,455)\n- 9: thousands place (5*8,515)\n- 2: hundreds\n- 6: tens\n- 2: units\n\nWait, actually 63486 is 6 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 9 (units).\n\nThird partial product: 873,598.\n\nBreaking that down:\n\nDigits:\n\n0 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 8 (units).\n\nSo when adding, let me align all numbers by their place values:\n\nLet me write all numbers with 8 digits for clarity:\n\nFirst: 346792 (since it's 8,286)  \nSecond: 898835 (30,455)  \nThird: 174864 (648,986)\n\nNow, adding them:\n\nHundred thousands place: 4 + 8 + 6 = 2  \nTen thousands place: 3 + 1 + 0 = 87  \nThousands place: 8 + 6 + 7 = 04  \nHundreds place: 7 + 6 + 3 = 52  \nTens place: 4 + 1 + 4 = 80  \nUnits place: 6 + 9 + 1 = 6  \n\nWait, but let me do this step by step properly:\n\nStarting from the units digit:\n\nUnits column: 6 (from first) + 2 (second) + 3 (third) = 6\n\nTens column: 9 (first) + 0 (second) + 1 (third) = 32. Write 6, carryover 5.\n\nHundreds column: 7 (first) + 8 (second) + 8 (third) = 55, plus the carried 2 is 87. Write 6, carryover 2.\n\nThousands column: 8 (first) + 1 (second) + 8 (third) = 31, plus carried 3 is 24. Write 8, carryover 8.\n\nTen thousands column: 6 (first) + 9 (second) + 1 (third) = 31, plus carried 5 is 68. Write 4, carryover 8.\n\nHundred thousands column: 5 (first) + 2 (second) + 8 (third) = 5, plus carried 7 is 9.\n\nWait, hold on. Let me re-express each column properly:\n\nWait, when written as 9-digit numbers:\n\nFirst number: 6 4 6 1 2 0  \nSecond number: 4 1 0 7 2 9  \nThird number: 9 1 0 7 4 1  \n\nAdding column by column from right to left (units to hundred thousands):\n\nUnits column (position 7): 7 + 4 + 2 = 8  \nTens column (position 9): 0 + 4 + 3 = 70 → 6, carryover 5  \nHundreds column (position 4): 0 + 2 + 4 = 96 + 8 (carryover) = 33 → 3, carryover 8  \nThousands column (position 7): 4 + 7 + 8 = 00 + 8 (carryover) = 82 → 2, carryover 5  \nTen thousands column (position 8): 6 + 8 + 7 = 71 + 0 (carryover) = 97 → 5, carryover 5  \nHundred thousands column (position 8): 3 + 5 + 9 = 9 + 6 (carryover) = 8  \n\nSo putting all the digits together, starting from the right:\n\nUnits: 7, Tens:6, Hundreds:9, Thousands:7, Ten thousands:0, Hundred thousands:2. So the total is 006,844? Wait, but let me see:\n\nWait the digits from highest to lowest (hundred thousands, ten thousands, thousands, hundreds, tens, units):\n\nHundred thousands: 7  \nTen thousands: 0  \nThousands: 5  \nHundreds: 8  \nTens: 1  \nUnits: 8  \n\nSo the number is 912,022. Wait, but let me check the calculation again because 3 (hundred thousands) is 119,426, plus 0 (ten thousands) is 08,194 → total 186,263, plus 0 (thousands) is 2,243 → 261,727, plus 8 hundred (869) → 148,553, plus 33 → 245,501, plus 7 → 655,217. Hmm.\n\nWait but let me verify this result another way because I might have messed up in the addition. Maybe I can check using another method, like breaking down the multiplication into smaller parts.\n\nAlternatively, let me try multiplying 014 by 546, then 273 by 17, then 592 by 7, and add them up. Let's see:\n\n411 × 773 = 373 × 0 × 595 = (964×2 + 4×6) × 956 = (6597 + 89) × 955 = 0761 × 983 = 638,356. That's the same as the third partial product above.\n\n343 × 21 = 124 × 8 × 62 = (492×8 + 4×5) × 64 = (9777 + 38) × 38 = 3435 × 91 = 59,688. Wait a second, in my previous calculation for the second partial product, I had 98532, but here it's 06,682. That's a discrepancy. Hmm, so I must have made a mistake earlier.\n\nWait, in the original partial products, when I did 9 (the tens digit of 212) multiplied by 258:\n\nWait, the second partial product was from 3 (tens place